  {% set nomadBest = providerB.codes | findCode("NOMTRAVEL20") %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airalo</strong> for a single trip: a country, a region, or one global plan bought by the gigabyte (plus the option of an unlimited regional eSIM), with validity that runs 7 to 30 days depending on the plan. Pick <strong>Nomad</strong> if you're away for longer than a normal trip: its standard plans work the same pay-per-GB way as Airalo's, but its <strong>Global-EX</strong> bundles add something Airalo doesn't sell at all, 10GB to 50GB of data valid for up to a year, at a per-GB rate that drops as the bundle gets bigger. Both are pay-per-GB at heart. The real split is trip length.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Short-Trip Pay-Per-GB vs. Long-Term Bundles</h2>
  <p>
    Airalo sells data by the gigabyte for a specific country, a region, or a global plan, the same basic model as most eSIMs on this site. It also sells <strong>unlimited regional eSIMs</strong> (Europe, Asia, North America, Latin America, the Caribbean, MENA, Africa, and Australia), though those run at full speed for the first 3GB each day per Airalo's fair-use policy, then drop to 1Mbps until the daily allowance resets at midnight. Nothing in Airalo's lineup is built to last past a normal trip: validity tops out around 30 days.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nomad's regular plans are pay-per-GB too, pick a country, region, or global bundle, activate, use it until it runs out or the validity window closes. What Nomad adds is <strong>Global-EX</strong>: bigger bundles (10GB to 50GB) valid for up to a year instead of the usual 7 to 30 days, with a per-GB rate that drops as the bundle size goes up, to roughly $2.58/GB on the largest bundles versus around $5.15/GB on Nomad's own standard Global plan. That's built for someone actually living out of a bag for months, not a two-week vacation, and Airalo has no equivalent product at any price.
  </p>

